# tools/ales_collapse_silence_witness.rish — the silence collapser: cap every silent run to a maximum, keeping the pauses yet shortening the long ones (ALES100).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES100.
# Design read: active-designing/20260814-235506_fill-ales100-lotus-silence-collapser.md
# Stands on: lotus/trim_silence.rye (ALES96 — the silence stripper, the zero-cap twin), lotus/segment.rye (ALES95 — the voice-activity segmenter), lotus/timeline.rye
# (ALES2 — the Clip and cut), lotus/voiced.rye (ALES94 — the .silent verdict). ALES96 removes EVERY silent sample; that is right for a sample library and wrong for a
# spoken take, where stripping all the pauses runs a conversation together. Every podcast editor keeps the pauses and caps only the long ones — truncate silence.
#
# lotus/collapse_silence.rye segments [0, clip.len) into frames of frame_len samples through ALES95, then walks the runs BACK TO FRONT and, for each .silent run longer
# than max_silence, cuts only the EXCESS (keeping the run's first min(count, max_silence) samples). The crux is two laws. THE ZERO-CAP LAW: collapse_silence(max=0)
# equals ALES96's trim_silence byte for byte. THE CAP LAW: the result equals the hand-built concatenation where each silent run keeps its first min(count, max_silence)
# samples; a cap past the longest run leaves the clip unchanged, monotone in max_silence. The selftest proves both, plus: a known cap keeps exactly the named samples;
# an all-silent clip caps its one coalesced run; a no-silent clip is unchanged; an empty clip, a ragged frame length, an inverted band, and an illegal floor each refuse
# by name, the clip untouched.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/ales_collapse_silence_witness.rish

say "ales-collapse-silence: Language EN — ALES96 removes EVERY silent sample, which is right for a sample library and wrong for a spoken take, where stripping all the pauses runs a conversation together, breathless, the natural rhythm gone. ALES98/99 handled the ends; this rung handles the INTERIOR the way a podcast editor works: keep the pauses, but cap the long ones. Truncate silence to a quarter-second is the single most-reached-for gesture in every voice-memo cleanup tool. The full strip is exactly max_silence = 0."
say "ales-collapse-silence: Style Radiant — the rule is stated positively: segment [0, clip.len) into frames, then walk the runs BACK TO FRONT and, for each .silent run longer than max_silence, cut only the EXCESS (keep the run's first min(count, max_silence) samples). The crux stands out as two laws: the zero-cap law (max=0 equals ALES96's full strip byte for byte) and the cap law (each silent run keeps min(count, max_silence) samples, a cap past the longest run leaves the clip unchanged, monotone in max)."
say "ales-collapse-silence: Lens TAME — one mono clip of i16 PCM segmented through ALES95 (all its validation runs before any cut); it edits only by removing whole excess-silent frames from within each silent run through ALES2's own cut, fabricating no sample and reordering nothing, never touching a non-silent sample or the kept head of any pause; max_silence is a plain u32 count with no illegal value; the error vocabulary is ALES96's TrimError (the segmenter's SegmentError and the editor's EditError) reused by name; siloed to lotus/, not a transcript or a real adaptive VAD, no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no device, no speaker."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "lotus/bin/collapse_silence"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p lotus/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/collapse_silence.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "ales-collapse-silence: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "lotus/bin/collapse_silence.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "ales-collapse-silence: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "ales-collapse-silence: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN ales-collapse-silence' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "ales-collapse-silence: selftest did not print the GREEN ales-collapse-silence line"
